@namespace Fhi.Munin.Explorer.Blazor

@* No @page — the host owns routing; in helsedata's Optimizely CMS there is no router at all.
   No @rendermode — the host decides at the mount site, which is what lets this same
   component render under legacy Blazor Server and under a modern Blazor Web App.
   No CSS ships from this package.

   Every class name below is one Fhi.Helsedata.Stiler already defines, and the shapes are
   lifted from helsedata's own datakildeutforsker (Features/DataSources/DataSourceComponent):
   a searchbox with the submit button inside the field, and results as a card list. Inventing
   `variabelutforsker-*` names — which is what this used to do — meant the host stylesheet had
   never heard of them, so the Søk button, the search field and the table headers rendered as
   raw browser defaults inside an otherwise styled page. Matching helsedata without guessing is
   the entire reason this ships as a component, so the names have to be theirs.

   Accessibility: helsedata.no is a public-sector site, so WCAG 2.1 AA is a legal
   requirement — and this is our markup on their page. The choices below are the ones
   markup can make. Visible focus and contrast are the host stylesheet's to get right, and
   are spelled out for hosts in the doc comment on the component class in the codebehind. *@

@* The one name here that is not Stiler's. It carries no styling — nothing in this package or
   in Stiler defines a rule for it — and exists so the component can be found in the DOM of a
   CMS page that renders a hundred other things. *@
<section class="variabelutforsker" aria-labelledby="@TittelId">
    @* Rendered from OverskriftNivaa rather than hard-coded to h2: the level that keeps the
       document outline unbroken is only knowable at the mount site. *@
    @Overskrift

    @* role="search" makes this a search landmark, and aria-labelledby names it after this
       instance's title so two explorers on one page are told apart in a landmark list. *@
    <form role="search"
          aria-labelledby="@TittelId"
          @onsubmit="SokAsync"
          @onsubmit:preventDefault="true">
        @* A visible label, deliberately, where helsedata's datakildeutforsker uses a bare
           aria-label. `form-element__label` is Stiler's own label style, so it costs nothing
           to keep the text on screen — and a visible label serves far more people than an
           invisible one. *@
        <label class="form-element__label" for="@SokId">@T.SokLedetekst</label>

        @* searchbox__freetext reserves 104px of right padding for a button sitting inside the
           field, which is what searchbox__freetext-submit-button positions there. Both come
           from Stiler; this is the arrangement their own search box uses. *@
        <div class="searchbox__freetext-container">
            @* @bind (onchange), deliberately NOT value + @oninput.

               This renders inside helsedata.no's Blazor Server circuit, so @oninput means one
               round-trip per keystroke — for a search that only ever runs on submit, that is
               traffic for nothing, multiplied by every visitor on a public site. Worse, each
               round-trip re-renders and rewrites the input while more input is still arriving:
               a fast paste or a programmatic fill loses characters (typing "svelging" quickly
               arrived as "sng").

               onchange is enough because searching is always an explicit act here: it fires on
               implicit submit (Enter), and a click on Søk blurs the field first, so the change
               event reaches the circuit before @onsubmit does. The event is spelled out rather
               than left implicit so nobody "helpfully" restores oninput. *@
            <input class="searchbox__freetext"
                   id="@SokId"
                   type="search"
                   @bind="_sok"
                   @bind:event="onchange"
                   placeholder="@T.SokPlassholder"
                   autocomplete="off"
                   maxlength="200" />
            @* Deliberately NOT disabled while a search runs. Disabling the element that
               currently has focus drops focus to <body>, so a keyboard user who just pressed
               Enter on Søk loses their place and starts tabbing from the top of the page
               again. SokAsync ignores a second submit while one is in flight instead, which
               stops the double request without touching focus. *@
            <button class="hd-button-square button-square--primary searchbox__freetext-submit-button"
                    type="submit">@T.SokKnapp</button>
        </div>
    </form>

    @* Ordering, as its own control rather than as clickable column headers.

       Runa sorts by clicking a <th>, with aria-sort on the header saying which way. There are no
       headers here — results are a datasourcecard list, because Stiler styles no table this
       package can reach — so neither the affordance nor aria-sort exists to copy. What the user
       actually needs is the ORDER, so it gets a control of its own above the list, and the chosen
       order is announced through the status line below instead of through aria-sort.

       fieldset + legend rather than a div with a label: the buttons are one choice made of several
       controls, and a legend is what names that group for a screen reader without inventing ARIA.
       `form-fieldset` (border and padding off) and `form-element__label` are both Stiler's.

       Always rendered, including when the search found nothing. Hiding it would take the button
       the user just pressed out of the document mid-interaction, dropping focus to <body> — the
       same reason the Søk button is never disabled. *@
    <fieldset class="form-fieldset">
        <legend class="form-element__label">@T.SortBy</legend>
        @foreach (var sort in Sortable)
        {
            @* Runa's semantics, minus the header: choosing the active field again reverses it,
               choosing another starts that one ascending.

               aria-current rather than aria-pressed. A pressed toggle promises that pressing again
               releases it, and this one does not — it flips the direction. aria-current="true" says
               what is true: this is the field the list is ordered by. The direction is in the
               button's own text, so it is on screen as well as announced, and no icon is needed —
               Stiler's sort icons are the ones its table headers use and they carry no text. *@
            <button class="@ButtonClass(sort)"
                    type="button"
                    aria-current="@AriaCurrent(sort)"
                    @onclick="() => SortAsync(sort)">@ButtonText(sort)</button>
        }
    </fieldset>

    @* One polite status line, holding one message at a time: loading, the result summary,
       or "no matches". aria-atomic makes a screen reader read the whole new sentence rather
       than only the words that changed — "12" on its own is not information.

       Loading is announced too. That is one extra utterance per search, which is the price
       of telling a screen-reader user that pressing Søk did something at all when the API
       is slow; the region is shared rather than stacked so the messages replace each other
       instead of piling up.

       `caption` is Stiler's muted secondary-text style. It also carries the empty state: a
       full-page emptystate block would be out of proportion inside a component that is one
       section of somebody else's page, and duplicating the sentence outside the live region
       would put it on screen twice. *@
    <p class="caption" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
        @if (_laster)
        {
            @T.Laster
        }
        else if (_resultat is { Items.Count: > 0 })
        {
            @Sammendrag
        }
        else if (_resultat is not null)
        {
            @T.IngenTreff(_utfortSok)
        }
    </p>

    @* The failure is worth interrupting for, so it is an alert rather than a polite update.
       The container is always rendered, empty when there is nothing wrong: a role="alert"
       element that is inserted and filled in the same DOM update is announced unreliably,
       while one that is already there when its text arrives is not. It carries no class of
       its own precisely because it is always present — an `infobox` here would draw an empty
       coloured box on every page that has nothing to report. *@
    <div role="alert" aria-live="assertive" aria-atomic="true">
        @if (_feil is not null)
        {
            <p class="infobox infobox--bg-yellow">@_feil</p>
        }
    </div>

    @if (_resultat is { Items.Count: > 0 })
    {
        @* A card list, not a table. Stiler styles no table this package could use — the only
           table rules it has are scoped to `article.registerOwnerListPage` — so a <table> here
           renders with 400-weight headers and no rules at all, which is exactly the raw-default
           look this change exists to remove. helsedata's own datakildeutforsker lists results as
           datasourcecards, so that is the shape adopted here: it needs no CSS from us, it wraps
           instead of scrolling sideways (which retires the focusable scroll box the table needed),
           and it drops the clipped <caption> in favour of a plain aria-label on the list.

           aria-label rather than aria-labelledby: the name is the same sentence the status line
           above shows, and pointing at that element would make the list's name change under a
           screen reader every time the status line does. *@
        <ul class="datasourcecard-list" aria-label="@Sammendrag" aria-busy="@Opptatt">
            @foreach (var v in _resultat.Items)
            {
                <li class="datasourcecard-list__item">
                    @* Stiler gives .datasourcecard a pointer cursor because on helsedata's page
                       the whole card is a link to a datasource page. Variables have no detail
                       URL in the Explorer API yet, so ours is not a link; when that endpoint
                       lands the heading becomes an anchor and the card is already right. *@
                    <div class="datasourcecard">
                        @RadOverskrift(v)
                        @Infolinje(v)
                        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(v.Beskrivelse))
                        {
                            <div class="datasourcecard__intro">
                                @* lang on the data itself, not on the whole list: the field
                                   labels around it follow Sprak, but Munin's descriptions are
                                   Norwegian whatever language the surrounding UI is in, and an
                                   English synthesiser reading Norwegian is unintelligible
                                   (WCAG 3.1.2). *@
                                <p lang="no">@v.Beskrivelse</p>
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>
                </li>
            }
        </ul>
    }
</section>
